.TH PAM_SSOOSSH 8 "2026-08-16" "ssoossh" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
pam_ssoossh \- PAM module for OIDC-approved SSH-certificate authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pam_ssoossh.so
.RB [ server = URL ]
.RB [ trusted-ca-file = PATH ]
.RB [ principals-map = PATH ]
.RB [ skew-tolerance = DURATION ]
.RB [ timeout = DURATION ]
.RB [ insecure-skip-verify ]
.RB [ debug [ =stdout ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam_ssoossh
authenticates a local PAM transaction (typically
.BR sudo (8)
or
.BR su (1))
by asking a human to approve it through the same ssoosshd server and OIDC
flow the interactive
.BR ssoossh (1)
client uses, rather than a Unix password.
.PP
On each authentication attempt the module:
.IP 1. 4
generates a fresh, single-use ed25519 keypair (nothing about it is
retained after the attempt completes);
.IP 2. 4
asks the configured server to certify it for the local account name PAM is
authenticating;
.IP 3. 4
displays the resulting approval URL through the PAM conversation (and logs
it at debug level as a fallback);
.IP 4. 4
waits, bounded by
.BR timeout ,
for the certificate to be issued over server-sent events; and
.IP 5. 4
validates the certificate it gets back against four checks before
succeeding (see
.B VALIDATION
below).
.PP
Only the
.B auth
PAM management group is implemented. The module's
.B setcred
entry point exists (required by libpam) but does nothing beyond reporting
success; do not reference
.B pam_ssoossh.so
on
.BR account ,
.BR password ,
or
.B session
lines.
.SH VALIDATION
A certificate is accepted only if all four hold:
.IP 1. 4
.B Signature.
It is signed by one of the CAs listed in
.BR trusted-ca-file ,
verified as a real signature, not a string comparison against the file's
contents.
.IP 2. 4
.B Key binding.
Its public key matches the ephemeral keypair generated for this attempt \(em
without this check, any certificate signed by a trusted CA and carrying the
right principal would be accepted, including one issued to somebody else's
key.
.IP 3. 4
.B Principal.
Its principals authorize the local account name PAM resolved (see
.BR principals-map ),
never an OIDC identity the module itself never sees.
.IP 4. 4
.B Validity window.
The current time falls within
.BR "[ValidAfter, ValidBefore]" ", with"
.B skew-tolerance
applied symmetrically to both ends.
.PP
Any failed check returns
.BR PAM_AUTH_ERR .
.SH MODULE ARGUMENTS
Arguments are
.IR key = value ,
or a bare
.I key
for a boolean flag. Parsing splits only on the first
.B =
so a value may itself contain one. Per
.BR pam.conf (5),
a value containing spaces must be bracketed \(em
.IR key =[a\~value\~with\~spaces] "  \(em"
libpam strips the brackets and joins the enclosed text into one argument
before this module ever sees it, so no further quoting is done here.
.TP
.BI server= URL
.B Required.
Base URL of the ssoosshd server, e.g.
.IR https://ssoosshd.example.com .
Missing or empty fails every authentication attempt with
.BR PAM_USER_UNKNOWN ,
before a keypair is generated or a network call is made.
.TP
.BI trusted-ca-file= PATH
.B Required.
Path to an
.BR authorized_keys (5)-format
file listing the CA(s) trusted to sign PAM certificates, one public key per
line. Rotate CAs by editing this file; no restart of the PAM-calling
process is needed, since it is re-read on every authentication attempt.
Missing, unreadable, unparseable, or empty (no keys) fails the attempt with
.BR PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA .
.TP
.BI principals-map= PATH
Path to a YAML file mapping a local account name to the certificate
principals allowed to assume it, e.g.:
.RS
.nf
alice:
  - alice
  - admin
.fi
.RE
When set and the file loads successfully, a certificate is accepted if any
of its principals is listed as allowed for the local account name. When
this argument is omitted, or set to a file that fails to load (missing
path, malformed YAML), the module instead requires an exact match: the
certificate's principals must literally contain the local account name.
A typo'd path or a corrupted map file therefore degrades to exact-match
behavior rather than locking every account out.
.TP
.BI skew-tolerance= DURATION
Clock-skew allowance applied to both ends of the certificate's validity
window, to absorb clock drift between this host and the server that issued
the certificate. Accepts Go duration syntax, e.g.
.IR 5s ", " 500ms .
Default and fallback (on an unparseable or absent value):
.BR 2s .
Choose this together with the server's
.B cert_options.pam.valid_duration
(see
.BR ssoosshd.yaml (5)):
too little slack rejects genuinely fresh certificates on hosts with modest
clock drift, too much widens the certificate's effective usable window past
its stated validity.
.TP
.BI timeout= DURATION
How long a single authentication attempt waits for browser approval before
giving up. Accepts Go duration syntax, e.g.
.IR 90s ", " 2m .
Default and fallback (on an unparseable or absent value):
.BR 60s .
A Ctrl-C at the prompt this module is authenticating (e.g. at a
.B sudo
password prompt) also abandons the attempt immediately, independent of this
timeout.
.TP
.B insecure-skip-verify
Skip TLS certificate verification when talking to
.BR server .
Accepted as a bare flag, or with an explicit boolean value (
.IR insecure-skip-verify=true ).
An unparseable value is treated as false. Default: false. Intended for
testing only; do not use it in a production PAM stack.
.TP
.BI debug [ =stdout ]
Controls the module's own debug-level logging, independent of the
version-and-outcome logging described under
.BR LOGGING .
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Absent, or set to
.BR false :
no debug logging (the default).
.IP \(bu 2
A bare
.B debug
flag, or any value other than
.B false
or
.BR stdout :
debug lines are written through syslog alongside the module's other log
output.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR debug=stdout :
debug lines are written to the process's stdout instead of syslog. On a
real
.B sudo
or
.B su
invocation stdout belongs to whatever invoked PAM, so this form is mainly
useful with the
.B pamtest
manual test harness (see
.IR pam_ssoossh/testing/ )
rather than production logins.
.RE
.SH LOGGING
The module logs through
.BR syslog (3)
using facility
.B LOG_AUTHPRIV
and tag
.RB \(dq ssoossh \(dq
(the same tag every ssoossh binary reports its identity under, not
.RB \(dq pam_ssoossh \(dq ).
If no syslog socket is reachable, it falls back to writing to the
process's stderr instead.
.PP
Every invocation logs its own version, commit, build date, and builder at
info level, unconditionally \(em not gated behind
.BR debug .
A successful authentication is logged at info level, naming the local
account. Failures are logged at error level with the reason. See
.B debug
above for the additional debug-level output that setting enables.
.SH RETURN VALUES
Values returned to libpam by this module's
.B pam_sm_authenticate
entry point, and the situations that produce each:
.TP
.B PAM_SUCCESS
All four validation checks passed.
.TP
.B PAM_USER_UNKNOWN
No PAM argument vector was supplied at all, the username could not be
retrieved from PAM, or
.B server
is unset.
.TP
.B PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA
.B trusted-ca-file
is unset, or the file it names could not be read or parsed.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
Any of the four validation checks failed (see
.BR VALIDATION );
the request was denied or expired before approval; the server reported it
could not issue the certificate; the wait for approval was interrupted or
timed out; or the server returned an unrecognized outcome.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL
The server could not be reached (any network error other than a timeout or
interruption). Deliberately distinct from
.BR PAM_AUTH_ERR :
an unreachable ssoosshd server is meant to let the PAM stack fall through
to whatever comes after this module's line, rather than making a server
outage an outage of every
.B sudo
on every host.
.TP
.B PAM_ABORT
An internal error unrelated to the server or the certificate (e.g. keypair
generation or certificate encoding failed).
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR pam_ssoossh.so
Module path, installed by the package:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
deb (x86_64):
.I /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/security/pam_ssoossh.so
.IP \(bu 2
deb (aarch64):
.I /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/security/pam_ssoossh.so
.IP \(bu 2
rpm (any arch):
.I /usr/lib64/security/pam_ssoossh.so
.RE
The package does not write a
.I /etc/pam.d
entry on install; an administrator adds the line explicitly (see
.B EXAMPLES
below).
.TP
.I docs/pam.d-sudo.example
Annotated example PAM stack entry shipped in the ssoossh source tree.
.SH EXAMPLES
Minimal, in
.IR /etc/pam.d/sudo ,
above the existing
.B pam_unix.so
line:
.RS
.nf
auth  sufficient  pam_ssoossh.so  server=https://ssoosshd.example.com  trusted-ca-file=/etc/ssoossh/ca.pub
.fi
.RE
.B sufficient
means a successful browser approval satisfies the
.B auth
stack on its own;
.B pam_unix.so
still runs afterward and can satisfy it independently (e.g. a local
emergency password) unless removed.
.PP
With the optional settings also in use:
.RS
.nf
auth  sufficient  pam_ssoossh.so  server=https://ssoosshd.example.com \e
    trusted-ca-file=/etc/ssoossh/ca.pub \e
    principals-map=/etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml \e
    skew-tolerance=5s timeout=90s
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam.conf (5),
.BR pam.d (5),
.BR pam_unix (8),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR authorized_keys (5),
.BR ssoosshd (8),
.BR ssoosshd.yaml (5),
.BR ssoossh (1)
